---
title: 'épisode 9 : au quotidien'
tags:
  - fr
  - language
  - learning
---

C: In [[fr/episode-9]], the present can express an [action in progress], a [habit], or a [description or general fact].

---

C: `On` commonly replaces `[nous]` in speech, but takes the third-person singular: `On [écoute] la radio.`

---

Q: Which plural pronoun represents a mixed-gender group?
A: `ils`. `elles` is reserved for an all-female group.

---

Q: What is the book's exact `Dans cet épisode…` teaser for épisode 9?
A: `Maryse, une amie d'Hélène, a un horaire bien rempli.`

---

Q: What are the two sentence patterns pairing a destination with a means of transport in épisode 9's header photos?
A: `aller` + destination + `en/à` + transport (`Il va au travail en métro.`), or `prendre` + transport + `pour` + infinitive naming the destination (`Il prend le métro pour aller au travail.`). See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: How does `prendre le déjeuner` generalize the verb `prendre` beyond `prendre l'autobus` and `prendre l'apéro`?
A: `prendre` also takes a meal as its object, a synonym of `manger`: `prendre le déjeuner` = `manger le déjeuner`.

---

C: Regular `-er` endings: je `-[e]`, tu `-[es]`, il/elle/on `-[e]`, nous `-[ons]`, vous `-[ez]`, ils/elles `-[ent]`.

---

Q: Why do `je parle`, `tu parles`, and `ils parlent` normally sound alike?
A: Their written endings `-e`, `-es`, and `-ent` are silent. The stem `parl-` supplies the audible form.

---

C: `je` elides before a vowel or h muet: `[j'écoute]`, `[j'étudie]`, `[j'habite]`.

---

Q: French for wash the dishes?
A: `laver la vaisselle` or `faire la vaisselle`.

---

Q: French for send emails, using the Québec word?
A: `envoyer des courriels`.

---

Q: French for eat a snack?
A: `manger une collation`.

---

Q: French for arrive home?
A: `arriver à la maison`.

---

Q: Which preposition follows `jouer` for a game or sport?
A: `à`: `jouer aux cartes`, `jouer à des jeux vidéos`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Which preposition follows `jouer` for an instrument or music?
A: `de`: `jouer du piano`, `jouer de la musique`.

---

Q: How does the construction of `relaxer` commonly differ between Québec and francophone Europe?
A: Québec commonly uses intransitive `relaxer`, while francophone Europe more often uses pronominal `se relaxer`. `se détendre` is a synonym. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: French for do the laundry?
A: `faire le lavage` or `faire la lessive`.

---

Q: French for take a nap?
A: `faire une sieste`.

---

Q: French for do the grocery shopping?
A: `faire l'épicerie`.

---

Q: Québec `faire le lavage` vs France `faire la lessive`?
A: Both mean do the laundry; the first is ordinary Québec usage and the second is more frequent in France.

---

Q: French for exercise, using `faire`?
A: `faire de l'exercice`.

---

C: `faire` au présent: je/tu `[fais]`, il/elle/on `[fait]`, nous `[faisons]`, vous `[faites]`, ils/elles `[font]`.

---

Q: Why must `faire le ménage`, `faire une sieste`, and `faire de l'exercice` be learned as complete expressions?
A: The article changes with the expression and cannot be predicted from the generic English verb "do".

---

C: `aller` au présent: je `[vais]`, tu `[vas]`, il/elle/on `[va]`, nous `[allons]`, vous `[allez]`, ils/elles `[vont]`.

---

C: Masculine destination: `Je vais [au] cinéma.` (`à + le`)

---

C: Feminine destination: `Je vais [à la] bibliothèque.`

---

C: Destination beginning with a vowel or h muet: `Je vais [à l']hôpital.`

---

C: Plural destination: `Je vais [aux] toilettes.` (`à + les`)

---

Q: Which destination preposition introduces a person or profession?
A: `chez`: `chez mon frère`, `chez Lise`, `chez le coiffeur`, `chez la dentiste`.

---

Q: What does `Je vais chez le dentiste` locate?
A: The dentist's office through the person or profession, rather than naming the place itself.

---

Q: Which preposition does `pied` take in the episode's transport expression?
A: `à`: `à pied`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Which preposition does `vélo` take in the episode's transport expression?
A: `à`: `à vélo`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Which preposition introduces the episode's vehicle transport modes?
A: `en`: `en auto`, `en autobus`, `en train`, `en métro`, `en taxi`.

---

Q: In `Je vais au travail en autobus`, what do the two complements answer?
A: `au travail` answers where; `en autobus` answers how.

---

C: In [[fr/episode-9]], Québec French uses `[le déjeuner]` for breakfast, `[le diner]` for lunch, and `[le souper]` for the evening meal.

---

Q: What does `l'avant-midi` mean in the episode?
A: The part of the morning before noon. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

C: Drink partitives: `[du] café`, `[du] lait`, `[du] jus d'orange`, `[de l']eau`, `[du] vin`, `[de la] bière`.

---

Q: How does the episode say “have an aperitif”?
A: `prendre l'apéro`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: How does the episode say “snack between meals”?
A: `grignoter`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Where does `vers` go when approximating a time?
A: Directly before the time: `Je déjeune vers 8 h.`

---

Q: Which expression for “approximately” appears after `à 6 h` in the episode?
A: `environ`: `à 6 h, environ`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Which conversational expression for “approximately” appears after `à midi trente`?
A: `à peu près`: `à midi trente, à peu près`. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

C: `prendre` au présent: je/tu `[prends]`, il/elle/on `[prend]`, nous `[prenons]`, vous `[prenez]`, ils/elles `[prennent]`.

---

Q: Where can a clock-time phrase appear after the action?
A: At the end: `Je prends l'autobus à 7 h 30.` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: What punctuation follows a clock-time phrase placed at the start of a sentence?
A: A comma: `À 7 h 30, je prends l'autobus.` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

C: Relative to a meal: `[avant] le déjeuner`, `[pendant] le diner`, `[après] le souper`.

---

Q: `le mardi` vs `mardi`?
A: `le mardi` means every Tuesday, a habit; bare `mardi` means one specific Tuesday supplied by context.

---

Q: `le soir` vs `ce soir`?
A: `le soir` marks an evening habit; `ce soir` means tonight, a punctual period.

---

C: Demonstratives for a punctual period: `[ce] matin`, `[ce] soir`, `[cet] avant-midi`, `[cet] après-midi`, `[cette] semaine`.

---

Q: Why does the book use `cet après-midi` rather than `ce après-midi`?
A: The book uses the accepted masculine form of `après-midi`, and a masculine noun beginning with a vowel takes `cet`. General French also accepts the feminine. See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: What does `en fin de semaine` mean in Québec?
A: On the weekend. France normally says `le week-end`.

---

Q: In Québec oral French, what is the second `tu` in `Tu vas-tu au travail ?`
A: An invariant interrogative particle marking a closed question. The first `tu` is the subject pronoun.

---

Q: Why can Québec French say `Elle écoute-tu la télé ?`
A: Interrogative `-tu` is an invariant question particle, not a pronoun agreeing with the subject `elle`.

---

Q: With which conjugated subjects does the book exclude interrogative `-tu`?
A: `nous` and `vous`.

---

Q: How can informal speech ask whether someone goes to work on foot using rising intonation?
A: `Tu vas au travail à pied ?` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: How can a neutral `est-ce que` question ask whether someone goes to work on foot?
A: `Est-ce que tu vas au travail à pied ?` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: How can formal or written inversion ask whether someone goes to work on foot?
A: `Vas-tu au travail à pied ?` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: How can informal Québec speech ask whether someone goes to work on foot with interrogative `-tu`?
A: `Tu vas-tu au travail à pied ?` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Reformulate `Elle va au parc ce soir ?` with interrogative `-tu`.
A: `Elle va-tu au parc ce soir ?`

---

Q: Reformulate `Laves-tu la vaisselle ?` with interrogative `-tu`.
A: `Tu laves-tu la vaisselle ?`

---

Q: Reformulate `Est-ce qu'il va à l'école à vélo ?` with interrogative `-tu`.
A: `Il va-tu à l'école à vélo ?`

---

Q: Reformulate `Écoutes-tu la radio dans ton auto ?` with interrogative `-tu`.
A: `Tu écoutes-tu la radio dans ton auto ?`

---

C: `lire` au présent: je/tu `[lis]`, il/elle/on `[lit]`, nous `[lisons]`, vous `[lisez]`, ils/elles `[lisent]`.

---

Q: Written vs ordinary spoken negation of `Je vais au travail en métro`?
A: Written: `Je ne vais pas au travail en métro.` Spoken: `Je vais pas au travail en métro.`

---

C: After negation, the partitive `du` becomes `[de]`: `Je fais du vélo.` becomes `Je ne fais pas de vélo.` See [[fr/episode-9]].

---

Q: Why does `en métro` stay unchanged in `Je ne vais pas au travail en métro`?
A: It is a transport prepositional phrase, not a partitive or indefinite object.

---

Q: Is the sports chart in épisode 9 current participation data?
A: No. It reports a historical PMB/Réseau Veille Tourisme series for 2005, 2010, and 2015.
